    <slider x="260" step="1.0" y="648" max="3.0" width="240" display="outage-supplier" height="55" min="0.0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.0" variable="outage-supplier"></slider>
    <slider x="520" step="1.0" y="648" max="500.0" width="240" display="outage-start" height="55" min="1.0" direction="Horizontal" default="3.0" variable="outage-start" units="cycle"></slider>
    <slider x="260" step="1.0" y="712" max="20.0" width="240" display="outage-duration" height="55" min="1.0" direction="Horizontal" default="2.0" variable="outage-duration" units="cycles"></slider>
    <monitor x="520" precision="0" y="712" height="55" fontSize="12" width="240" display="Out this cycle">outage-status-text</monitor>
    <note x="780" y="592" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="13" width="300" markdown="false" height="29" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">Procurement costs</note>
    <monitor x="780" precision="2" y="628" height="62" fontSize="11" width="94" display="Purchase">period-purchase-cost</monitor>
    <monitor x="883" precision="2" y="628" height="62" fontSize="11" width="94" display="Transport">period-transport-cost</monitor>
    <monitor x="986" precision="2" y="628" height="62" fontSize="11" width="94" display="Link fees">period-relationship-cost</monitor>
    <monitor x="780" precision="2" y="700" height="62" fontSize="12" width="143" display="Period total">period-total-cost</monitor>
    <monitor x="937" precision="2" y="700" height="62" fontSize="12" width="143" display="Cumulative total">cumulative-total-cost</monitor>
    <note x="260" y="783" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="13" width="820" markdown="false" height="26" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">Random supplier outages</note>
    <note x="260" y="811" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="10" width="820" markdown="false" height="22" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">Each available supplier can fail after orders. 0% turns random outages off.</note>
    <slider x="260" step="1.0" y="840" max="100.0" width="190" display="outage-risk" height="55" min="0.0" direction="Horizontal" default="2.0" variable="outage-risk" units="%"></slider>
    <slider x="470" step="1.0" y="840" max="20.0" width="190" display="duration-min" height="55" min="1.0" direction="Horizontal" default="1.0" variable="duration-min" units="cycles"></slider>
    <slider x="680" step="1.0" y="840" max="20.0" width="190" display="duration-max" height="55" min="1.0" direction="Horizontal" default="10.0" variable="duration-max" units="cycles"></slider>
    <slider x="890" step="1.0" y="840" max="9999.0" width="190" display="scenario-seed" height="55" min="1.0" direction="Horizontal" default="2026.0" variable="scenario-seed"></slider>
    <note x="260" y="901" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="10" width="820" markdown="false" height="27" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">Same scenario seed repeats demands and random outages. Change sliders, then click Setup.</note>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## Purpose

Stage 6 adds independent random supplier outages and automatic recovery alongside the optional scheduled outage. The network still has three suppliers and three manufacturers. Each manufacturer splits its order equally among one, two, or three fixed suppliers. Purchase, transport, and relationship costs remain in the model. Inventory, supplier switching, and changing prices come later.

## Network and controls

The supplier-count slider sets the number of suppliers used by *each* manufacturer. Setup selects the first one, two, or three suppliers in these fixed preference lists:

| Manufacturer | First | Second | Third |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| M1 | S1 | S2 | S3 |
| M2 | S2 | S3 | S1 |
| M3 | S1 | S3 | S2 |

The supplier-capacity slider sets each supplier's capacity. The min-demand and max-demand sliders give all manufacturers the same inclusive demand range; Setup draws one integer demand per manufacturer. Demand, capacity, and relationships stay fixed during a run. The scenario-seed slider fixes these demand draws and the random outage events. The same scenario seed and demand range give identical manufacturer demands across repeated Setups and all three sourcing strategies; change the seed for a new scenario.

Positions are fixed for readability and have no effect on transportation cost. Each potential manufacturer–supplier pair instead has a unit transportation rate. Setup draws all nine rates from 0.5–1.5 cost units per delivered mineral unit, rounded to two decimal places. The route-seed slider (1–9999) selects a reproducible rate table. The same seed produces the same rates for all three sourcing strategies, including pairs without an active link. Changing the seed and clicking Setup generates a different scenario. The rate table stays fixed during a run.

## Controlled outage

Set outage-supplier to 0 for no outage, or to 1, 2, or 3 to interrupt S1, S2, or S3. Outage-start is the first affected cycle; outage-duration is the number of affected cycles. Cycle 1 is the first Step after Setup. For example, start = 3 and duration = 2 interrupt cycles 3 and 4, and the supplier recovers in cycle 5.

The supplier receives its usual orders before the outage takes effect in every affected cycle. Its available capacity and deliveries are then zero throughout the selected interval. Normal capacity returns when the interval ends unless a random outage is still active. There is only one scheduled outage per run.

## Random outages

Outage-risk is the independent chance, in percent, that each currently available supplier fails in a cycle. The default is 2%; set it to 0% to turn random outages off. After orders have been submitted, a new failure reduces that supplier's deliverable capacity to zero in the current cycle. Its duration is drawn uniformly from the inclusive duration-min to duration-max range; the defaults are 1 and 10 cycles. A failure starting in cycle 3 with duration 2 affects cycles 3 and 4; the supplier becomes eligible to operate normally again in cycle 5. On that first eligible cycle it can also draw a new failure, so a high risk can produce consecutive outages.

Suppliers already in either a scheduled or random outage do not draw a new failure. A random outage already in progress continues counting down during a scheduled outage. If the two intervals overlap, the supplier is unavailable until both have ended. Each supplier-and-cycle draw uses scenario-seed, independently of sourcing strategy and route-seed. With the same controls, Setup reproduces the same demands and outage sequence. Multiple suppliers can be out in one cycle.

Established relationships and equal order shares do not change; manufacturers do not redirect orders to other suppliers in this stage.

## Each cycle

1. Each manufacturer splits its full demand equally among its contract links.
2. After orders are submitted, scheduled and random outages make affected suppliers' available capacity zero. Other suppliers retain normal capacity. Each supplier applies one fill ratio to its incoming links.
3. A link's purchase charge is its delivered units × the supplier's unit price. Its transport charge is delivered units × that pair's transport rate. Both charges are zero when nothing is delivered.
4. Each manufacturer also pays 50 cost units per active supplier relationship every cycle, even if that supplier delivers nothing. Its total cost is purchase + transport + relationship cost.
5. The model updates current-cycle and cumulative cost and demand totals. One tick is one procurement cycle.

Every supplier's unit purchase price is 100 cost units in this stage. Supplier-specific prices and price feedback are later stages. Unmet demand is reported separately; it is not assigned a monetary penalty.

## Reading the display

Click Setup to build the network, Step for one cycle, or Run for continuous cycles. Changing any slider requires another Setup. Setup stops with an error if min-demand exceeds max-demand or duration-min exceeds duration-max.

Green boxes are available suppliers; a red supplier marked OUT is interrupted by either mechanism. Blue houses are manufacturers; orange houses have unmet demand. An orange link was only partly delivered because of capacity, while a red link leads to an interrupted supplier. Supplier labels show `orders received → amount shipped`; manufacturer labels show `amount received / demand`. Out this cycle lists every interrupted supplier after each Step and shows None when all are available.

The upper right monitors show period demand, deliveries, unmet demand, and fill rates. The lower right monitors show current-cycle purchase, transport, and relationship charges, their period total, and the cumulative total across completed cycles. Fill rate is delivered / demand × 100%; it shows N/A when demand is zero.

## Hand-check example

Set supplier-capacity to 50, min-demand and max-demand to 30, and route-seed to 2026. The route rates, with manufacturers as rows and suppliers as columns, are:

| Route | S1 | S2 | S3 |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| M1 | 0.72 | 0.91 | 1.48 |
| M2 | 0.59 | 0.98 | 1.49 |
| M3 | 0.70 | 1.41 | 1.06 |

With supplier-count = 1, S1 ships 50 and S2 ships 30, for 80 delivered and 10 unmet. Purchase cost is 8,000, transport cost is 64.90, and three relationship fees total 150, so period total cost is 8,214.90. With supplier-count = 2, all 90 units are delivered; purchase, transport, and relationship costs are 9,000, 87.90, and 300, for a period total of 9,387.90. With supplier-count = 3, all 90 units are delivered and the three costs are 9,000, 93.40, and 450, for a period total of 9,543.40.

For a scheduled outage check, first set outage-risk = 0, then keep supplier-count = 1 and set outage-supplier = 1, outage-start = 3, and outage-duration = 2. S1 still receives 60 units of orders in cycles 3 and 4 but ships zero. Network deliveries fall to 30, unmet demand rises to 60, and period total cost is 3,179.40, including 150 in relationship fees. In cycle 5, S1 recovers and the normal 80 deliveries and 8,214.90 period cost return.

For a random outage check, set outage-supplier = 0, outage-risk = 100, and duration-min = duration-max = 1. Every supplier fails after orders in every cycle; deliveries, purchase charges, and transport charges are zero, while relationship fees remain payable.]]></info>
